00:04:28.799 --> 00:04:31.703
- Steadman and I were in a hotel

00:04:31.703 --> 00:04:33.688
watching some bad television.

00:04:33.688 --> 00:04:37.502
And I was complaining about
the state of television,

00:04:37.502 --> 00:04:39.250
talk shows in particular.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.569
And he said "why don't you change it?"

00:04:40.569 --> 00:04:42.599
Why don't you just get your own network?

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:45.214
and I thought that that
was a ridiculous idea.

00:04:45.214 --> 00:04:47.077
And then he started to talk about it,

00:04:47.077 --> 00:04:48.802
and he wrote some things down.

00:04:48.802 --> 00:04:51.716
And I saw the word own and I thought

00:04:51.716 --> 00:04:55.519
own, ow, oh that's my,
those are my initials.

00:04:55.519 --> 00:04:57.567
And I said, wouldn't it be
something if I could create

00:04:57.567 --> 00:05:00.535
a network, that was really about mindful,

00:05:00.535 --> 00:05:03.702
and I underlined the
word mindful, television.

00:05:03.702 --> 00:05:06.373
- [Interviewer] 15 years
later, those scribblings came

00:05:06.373 --> 00:05:08.935
closer to a reality when
she was approached by

00:05:08.935 --> 00:05:11.817
David Zaslav, the president
of the Discovery network.

00:05:11.817 --> 00:05:13.551
- My view was, if we want to do this,

00:05:13.551 --> 00:05:14.681
you have to be all in.

00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:16.334
She paused,

00:05:16.334 --> 00:05:20.506
and then she said to me
"yeah, it's meant to be."

00:05:20.506 --> 00:05:23.880
And we shook hands, and
that's how our journey began.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.753
- At first I thought
well great the network,

00:05:25.753 --> 00:05:27.518
oh gee this is the dream I had.

00:05:27.518 --> 00:05:29.512
And I actually showed him the
piece of paper where I had

00:05:29.512 --> 00:05:31.351
written it down in segments.

00:05:31.351 --> 00:05:34.989
And as that started to settle in with me,

00:05:34.989 --> 00:05:36.757
I thought whoa

00:05:36.757 --> 00:05:39.013
what is this I've gotten myself into?

00:05:39.013 --> 00:05:41.926
This is a lot more work
than I ever imagined.

00:05:41.926 --> 00:05:43.215
- [Interviewer] Are you scared?

00:05:43.215 --> 00:05:45.788
- Very scared. I was very scared.

00:05:45.788 --> 00:05:47.788
And I would wake up in
the middle of the night,

00:05:47.788 --> 00:05:48.723
and I would literally

00:05:48.723 --> 00:05:49.645
(gasps)

00:05:49.645 --> 00:05:52.276
like clutching my chest,
like what have I done.

00:05:52.276 --> 00:05:53.971
What have I done?

00:05:53.971 --> 00:05:55.280
- [Interviewer] What if you fail?

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.526
- That's what I was afraid of.

00:05:57.526 --> 00:05:59.265
That's what I was afraid of.

00:05:59.265 --> 00:06:03.270
The reason why I made the
decision to go forward,

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.603
is because I believe that people deserve

00:06:08.706 --> 00:06:13.247
to have value centered
inspirational programming

00:06:13.247 --> 00:06:14.496
for themselves.

00:06:14.496 --> 00:06:18.663
I believe that television
has become in many ways,

00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:21.511
a null.

00:06:21.511 --> 00:06:25.678
It has become (inaudible)
and not all of it,

00:06:29.242 --> 00:06:31.537
but I think that I have
something to offer.

00:06:31.537 --> 00:06:33.186
- [Interviewer] What
circumstances were present

00:06:33.186 --> 00:06:36.488
that made this idea catch fire.

00:06:36.488 --> 00:06:38.880
- Sure so I think there
were sort of two components.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.276
And the first component is
probably that social media

00:06:42.276 --> 00:06:43.772
was starting to feel a little stale.

00:06:43.772 --> 00:06:47.251
There's only so many beautiful,
attractive, cool pictures

00:06:47.251 --> 00:06:49.623
that you can take and
when you see all of them,

00:06:49.623 --> 00:06:52.069
day after day after day, it
just kind of gets boring.

00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:54.435
So I think there was that aspect of it.

00:06:54.435 --> 00:06:57.468
And I think the other thing
is that we really identified

00:06:57.468 --> 00:07:01.751
that this femoral thing
was core to conversation.

00:07:01.751 --> 00:07:03.542
And so, if you wanted
to have a conversation,

00:07:03.542 --> 00:07:06.295
one of the best parts about
it was that you were left with

00:07:06.295 --> 00:07:09.009
the memory but not
necessarily a transcript.

00:07:09.009 --> 00:07:14.003
And I think that that really
drove the great feeling that

00:07:14.003 --> 00:07:16.308
you have when you share snaps
with friends and they're

00:07:16.308 --> 00:07:19.859
silly and you know, not exactly perfect.

00:07:19.859 --> 00:07:22.285
You know there was obviously
a place for all of these

00:07:22.285 --> 00:07:23.976
great photos that you
wanted to save and keep.

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:26.761
And there wasn't so much a
place to have a conversation.

00:07:26.761 --> 00:07:29.710
And I think you know, there's
a nice balance with both.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:32.282
And so I think we sort of filled a void,

00:07:32.282 --> 00:07:34.011
the conversation void that was missing.

00:07:34.011 --> 00:07:35.996
- I just think music is changing

00:07:35.996 --> 00:07:38.286
in terms of the gate keepers
and the power that be,

00:07:38.286 --> 00:07:40.496
the powers that be.

00:07:40.496 --> 00:07:44.984
And I feel like it's in
a more democratic time.

00:07:44.984 --> 00:07:49.056
The power is in the musician's
hands and in the fan's hands.

00:07:49.056 --> 00:07:53.139
I mean they choose what
matters, what's relevant,

00:07:54.952 --> 00:07:56.778
what sells, what you know.

00:07:56.778 --> 00:07:58.743
It's not so much

00:07:58.743 --> 00:08:02.525
the old powers, the old
gate keepers who could just

00:08:02.525 --> 00:08:05.275
shove something down your throat.

00:08:07.468 --> 00:08:11.231
You see less and less
superstars and more and more

00:08:11.231 --> 00:08:14.398
organically grown artists like myself,

00:08:15.536 --> 00:08:19.235
just doing something a little
different, know what I mean.

00:08:19.235 --> 00:08:23.536
Bending genres and don't
necessarily fit within this like

00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:26.786
format or walls of you know, whatever.

00:08:27.691 --> 00:08:29.273
Just kind of doing their
own thing and finding an

00:08:29.273 --> 00:08:32.827
audience and growing
it organically and just

00:08:32.827 --> 00:08:34.244
making it happen.

